= An Envoy from the North

An envoy from the Empress has arrived in Axton. He brings no ultimatum, only courtesies: a wreath at the Founding memorial and a round of dinners with the capital's leading men. The purpose of the visit is clear to everyone from the Foreign Ministry to the gutter press. The Empire is assessing the Commonwealth's condition.

[? if empire_posture >= 65: He appears satisfied with what he finds. The Empress's faction is ascendant at the northern court, and its language toward the republic has become conciliatory. Its spokesmen talk of partnership and of healing old wounds. This warmth should not be mistaken for friendship. ?][? if empire_posture >= 40 and empire_posture < 65: His courtesies are correct and his questions precise. At the northern court, we are told, the Empress's conciliators and the Hardliners still compete for her ear, and nothing in the Commonwealth's condition has yet settled the argument either way. ?][? if empire_posture < 40: The courtesies are thin. The Hardliners hold the northern court, and their view of the republic is no secret: a weak and quarrelsome remnant, holding what the Empire intends to reclaim. ?]

[? if reunification_support >= 50: The Foreign Ministry is more troubled by the reception than by the envoy himself. In the salons, in the propertied press, and even in some guild halls, talk of reunion with the north is no longer treated as absurd. ?][? if reunification_support < 50: Talk of reunion is still confined to Royalist salons and a few nostalgic colonels, but the envoy's hosts took careful note of who attended his dinners. ?][? if imperial_fear >= 5: In the streets the visit is received differently. The north means invasion scares, and the scares are becoming easier to start. ?]

The Convention will settle the question of the north one way or another. The envoy's report will help decide on whose terms.
